![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

The current implementation of `glob_match` allocates two `Vec<&str>` on every call by collecting the split segments. Since `glob_match` is called thousands of times during classification and alignment checks, these allocations can be completely avoided by comparing the split iterators on the fly.

```rust
pub fn glob_match(pattern: &str, name: &str) -> bool {
    let mut p_iter = pattern.split('.');
    let mut n_iter = name.split('.');
    loop {
        match (p_iter.next(), n_iter.next()) {
            (Some(p), Some(n)) => {
                if !segment_match(p, n) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
            (None, None) => return true,
            _ => return false,
        }
    }
}
```

